menu "Audio conversion (audio_convert)"

config AUDIO_CONVERT_MP3
    bool "Support MP3 uploads (vendors minimp3)"
    default n
    help
        Lets Settings -> Operator / Dial Codes accept .mp3 uploads directly
        from the browser, decoded on-device via minimp3 (CC0) before going
        through the same 8kHz-mono conversion as WAV uploads. WAV upload
        works either way; MP3 sources also always work via the host-side
        sync_pi_audio.py/gen_prompt.py --from song.mp3 workflow regardless
        of this option.

        Measured costs of turning this on (your own board -- rebuild and
        compare if you want exact numbers):
          - Flash: modest, ~23KB (minimp3 itself is small).
          - RAM: NOT modest. minimp3's mp3dec_decode_frame() declares a
            ~16KB local (its internal decode scratch buffers) -- confirmed
            via a live "Stack protection fault" panic + addr2line, not a
            guess. The web UI's httpd task stack has to be sized well past
            that single local (32KB total, up from 8KB otherwise) to
            avoid crashing on every MP3 upload, and that's a permanent
            cost of the one always-running httpd worker task, not
            something paid only during an upload. On a chip with ~342KB
            total usable RAM, that's the real tradeoff to weigh, not the
            flash number.

endmenu
